    {
      "dom_id": "question-181669",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi )=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2q\\pi )=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2},\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2}\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
