  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "dom_id": "step-152953",
      "name": "Example: \n                    Sets Defined Using Inequalities",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14122998/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": null,
