    {
      "dom_id": "step-152959",
      "name": "Example: \n                    Sets Defined Using Derivatives",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
